// ALG1 Unit 04 Lesson 04 Guided Practice
// Lesson: Domain and Range
// MLA Standard: Domain and Range Conceptual Support - no new primary MLA standard assigned in approved mapping
::ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
The ordered pairs (1,4), (2,6), and (3,8) represent a function. What is the domain?
{
~A. (4, 6, 8)#These are the output values, so they form the range. The domain is the set of input values.
=B. (1, 2, 3)#The domain is the set of x-values: 1, 2, and 3.
~C. (1, 4, 2, 6, 3, 8)#This lists both inputs and outputs. The domain includes only the input values.
~D. (1, 3, 4, 8)#This leaves out some values and mixes inputs with outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
The ordered pairs (1,4), (2,6), and (3,8) represent a function. What is the range?
{
~A. (1, 2, 3)#These are the input values, so they form the domain. The range is the set of output values.
~B. (1, 4, 2, 6, 3, 8)#This lists inputs and outputs together. The range includes only outputs.
~C. (2, 4, 6)#This list does not match the y-values in the ordered pairs.
=D. (4, 6, 8)#The range is the set of y-values: 4, 6, and 8.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
A table shows x-values 0, 1, 2 and corresponding f(x)-values 10, 15, 20. Which statement correctly identifies the domain and range?
{
=A. Domain: (0, 1, 2); Range: (10, 15, 20)#The domain is the set of inputs, and the range is the set of outputs.
~B. Domain: (10, 15, 20); Range: (0, 1, 2)#This reverses the input and output sets.
~C. Domain: (0, 10); Range: (2, 20)#This only uses the first and last pairs and misses middle values.
~D. Domain: (0, 1, 2, 10, 15, 20); Range: (0, 1, 2, 10, 15, 20)#This combines all values instead of separating inputs and outputs.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
A ticket function uses t for the number of tickets bought. Which domain is reasonable for this context?
{
~A. All real numbers#Ticket counts cannot include decimals or negative values in this context.
~B. All negative integers#A person cannot buy a negative number of tickets.
=C. Whole numbers such as 0, 1, 2, 3, ...#Ticket counts are nonnegative whole numbers.
~D. Only the numbers between 0 and 1#A person may buy more than one ticket, so this domain is too limited.
}

::ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID: ALG1_U04_L04_GP_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A1.FN.1
Question:
A container can hold at most 30 gallons of water. Which range is reasonable for the amount of water in the container?
{
~A. All real numbers#The amount of water cannot be negative or exceed the container capacity.
=B. 0 to 30 gallons, inclusive#The container can be empty, full, or hold any amount between 0 and 30 gallons.
~C. Only 30 gallons#The container can hold less than its maximum capacity.
~D. 30 gallons or more#Amounts greater than 30 gallons exceed the capacity.
}

